Z||000023||fropuff||08-10-2001||01:25 AM||||Oh, they do!  What happened was this: there was a cop doing a speed trap facing the opposite direction on a cross street from a highway.  A guy on the highway must've been going 10 over tops. (in a 55 zone).  The cop's brake lights flashed, and his tires started a smokin'.  He whipped a 180 and crossed two lanes of oncoming traffic, making cars dynamite the brakes.  He then side-steered onto the guy's lane and pulled him over.  For ten over.  Let's see...a $67 ticket, or 3+ fatalities?  Pissed me off.  ||||reg||1
Z||000024||Cameron||08-10-2001||03:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>LMAO, thats a great one. the one flaw in that is its federal crime to take photos of money. so you cant win.</B><BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You can take pictures of money as long as the bill is folded...so it can't be considered a forgery.<P>There is no way something like that would last too long in a car...and besides...take it out...a car is required by law to pass smog, but there is no way that a law forcing people to leave a gps tracking device in their car.<P>Its like the fact that its not legal for the government to charge you for registration, but the fees from lawyers to fight it make it just plain cheaper and easier to pay the registration.  You paid for the vehicle, you pay for the roads, you pay for the cops, they can't force you to pay for your vehicle more then once.<P>------------------<BR>Cameron<BR> <A HREF="http://Fiero.NetMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>http://Fiero.NetMARC.com</A> ||||reg||1
